# Ultrasensitive Plasmonic Immunosensor Based on Hierarchically Porous Metal–Organic Framework‐Engineered Enzyme Catalysts

## abstract
Investigations on the utilization efficiency and bioactivity of the enzyme nanocarriers are critical for realizing sensitive biosensors. Herein, hierarchically porous enzyme@peptide‐directed metal–organic frameworks (enzyme@MOFs‐P) nanoarchitectures are constructed by implemented site‐selective epitaxial growth to create mesopore and defect structures. The expanded size of the pores provides suitable space for homogeneously accommodating enzymes and enhances interfacial contact with enzymes, as well as reducing steric hindrance of target recognition to boost the mass transfer process. Harnessing the enzyme‐mediated surface etching of gold nanostars, a robust enzyme@MOFs‐P‐based plasmonic immunosensor is constructed for the sensitive detection of imidacloprid pesticide. In conjunction with using a smartphone‐based homemade device and image‐processing algorithm, quantitative detection of imidacloprid is achieved with a half maximal inhibitory concentration of 0.02 ng mL−1, which shows ≈1000‐fold improvement in sensitivity compared with that of a typical immunoassay. This work gives valuable insight into engineering size‐tunable nanostructures and the structure‐activity relationship of MOFs, guiding the construction of plasmonic biosensors for on‐site application.

Investigations on the utilization efficiency and bioactivity of the enzyme nanocarriers are critical for realizing sensitive biosensors. Herein, hierarchically porous enzyme@peptide-directed metal–organic frameworks (enzyme@MOFs-P) nanoarchitectures are constructed by implemented site-selective epitaxial growth to create mesopore and defect structures. The expanded size of the pores provides suitable space for homogeneously accommodating enzymes and enhances interfacial contact with enzymes, as well as reducing steric hindrance of target recognition to boost the mass transfer process. Harnessing the enzyme-mediated surface etching of gold nanostars, a robust enzyme $@$ MOFs-P-based plasmonic immunosensor is constructed for the sensitive detection of imidacloprid pesticide. In conjunction with using a smartphone-based homemade device and image-processing algorithm, quantitative detection of imidacloprid is achieved with a half maximal inhibitory concentration of $0.02\mathrm{\ng\}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , which shows ${\approx}1000$ -fold improvement in sensitivity compared with that of a typical immunoassay. This work gives valuable insight into engineering size-tunable nanostructures and the structure-activity relationship of MOFs, guiding the construction of plasmonic biosensors for on-site application.
## 1. Introduction
Plasmonic immunosensor, as a promising and burgeoning technique, has garnered immense attention in the fields of disease diagnosis and environmental monitoring.[1,2] The generation of absorbance-based response per a single antibody-recognition event determines the sensitivity at the signal-transducing stage of the plasmonic immunosensor, making it imperative to boost the corresponding signals for sensitive detection of the target. Enzyme as signal amplifier faces formidable challenges in poor bioactivity and stability parameters toward harsh surrounding, resulting in the degradation of sensitivity.[3,4] In a basic approach, instead of enzyme molecule tagging in the immunosensor, nanocarriers including hybrid organic–inorganic nanoflower,[5] ferritin nanoparticles,[6] proteinosome,[7,8] and extracellular vesicle[9] are used as probes to improve the sensitivity by enriching a large number of signaling molecules. Despite these advancements in accumulating biomacromolecules, the apparent activities of the immobilized enzyme are usually low due to the poor enzyme activity and utilization efficiency.[10] Seeking appropriate scaffolds to load enzymes and maintain their activity, particularly possessing high utilization efficiency, is desperate for the fabrication of high-performance plasmonic immunosensor.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted wide interest for enzyme immobilization, which not only can achieve high-mass enrichment of enzymes with good stability in storage and operational conditions, but also provide flexible channels for substrate penetration to improve enzyme utilization efficiency.[11–15] However, because of the constrained enzyme conformation in MOFs, the immobilized enzyme experiences decreased activity.[16] The intrinsic pores of most MOFs are typically small in the microporous range $(<2\ \mathrm{nm})$ , limiting the ingress of substrates and mass transfer.[17] In typical MOF systems, achieving high activity of enzyme $@\mathrm{MOFs}$ is difficult without adjusting the spatial distribution of the immobilized enzymes and mass-transfer capacity of the substrate. Extensive efforts have been dedicated to enhancing the catalytic activity of immobilized enzymes through pore voids enlargement,[18,19] structure disintegrate-released ion activators,[13] enzyme embedding patterns modulation,[20] and the hydrophilicity microenvironment control.[21] Those highly efficient strategies integrate enzyme immobilization of high stability and homogeneous biocatalysis that allows for unrestricted diffusion, which facilitates the improvement of immobilized enzyme activity and binding quantity toward the substrate, ultimately the sensitivity of the platform. It is worth noting that creating mesopores inside the hierarchically porous MOFs allows for the encapsulation of large-sized enzymes in their relatively free state while increasing substrate diffusion. There is a “trade-off” relationship between the framework integrity and the microporosity, easily causing the collapse or partial amorphization of the MOFs framework. Therefore, rational adjustment of the size distribution of pores to match the demand for the enzymecatalyzed reaction is deemed to boost the enzyme activity and utilization efficiency and subsequently improve the performance of the plasmonic immunosensor.
Herein, a hierarchically porous enzyme $@$ peptide-directed metal–organic frameworks (enzyme $@$ MOFs-P) nanoarchitecture was constructed by introducing γ-poly(l-glutamic acid) (PLGA) peptide surface modifier (Scheme  1). The PLGA peptide chain with abundant carboxylic groups can modify the enzyme and coordinate with metal ions to trigger morphological evolution from rhombohedron dodecahedron to nanostars, accompanying the generation of the mesopore and defect structures in the composites. Expanding the pore size of the MOFs-P provides sufficient space for enzyme immobilization and accelerates the diffusion of substrates and intermediates, which facilitates intercommunication. For immunosensor, the antibody-labeled enzyme $@$ MOFs-P possesses specialized recognition capacity for the antigen and enzyme-modulated signal amplification capacity. Harnessing plasmonic colorimetry, the enzyme label of immunosensor protocol controls the surface etching of gold nanostars to generate colored solutions with distinct tonality. As a proof-of-principle, imidacloprid pesticide is chosen as the model target to demonstrate the ultrasensitive performance of the enzyme $@$ MOFs-P-based plasmonic immunosensor with a distinguishable blue-to-red color change. Through an image-processing algorithm, three primary color codes (RGB: red, green, and blue) enable precise quantitative analysis in combination with a smartphone-based homemade device. The successful integration of enzyme $@$ MOFs-P with plasmonic immunosensor and image-processing algorithm enables on-site detection of pesticides with 1000-fold enhanced sensitivity. Such an investigation realizes visual inspection of trace targets and possesses broad application prospects of the rational design size-controlled nanoarchitectures for advanced biosensors.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Morphology Evolution of HRP@MOFs-P
Hierarchically porous MOFs-P are prepared by the coprecipitation approach depending on the strong combination between $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ nodes and 2-methylimidazole (HmIM) in the presence of PLGA peptide, which in situ encapsulated HRP (Figure 1a). PLGA peptide can modify HRP via electrostatic interaction to coordinate with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ which disrupts the original $Z\mathrm{n}$ -HmIM tetrahedron arrangement and accelerates the formation of ${\mathrm{HRP}}@{\mathrm{MOFs-P}}$ (Figures  S1–S4, Supporting Information). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image displays the morphological evolution of the MOFs-P from dodecahedron to nanostar-like morphology (Figure  1b; Figure  S5, Supporting Information). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping proved evidence of uniform dispersion of C, N, O, S, and Zn elements throughout the entire architecture of HRP $@$ MOFs-P (Figure  1c,d). An enzyme adsorption experiment is carried out to elucidate that the enzyme biomolecule is encapsulated in situ rather than post-adsorbed on MOFs-P (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Additional evidence of in situ encapsulation is garnered by using HRP tagged with fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC-HRP) to induce the formation of hierarchically porous MOFs-P. The green fluorescent localization of FITC-HRP is distributed within the HRP $@$ MOFs-P nanoarchitectures through Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) (Figure  1e), underpinning the homogeneous accommodation of HRP in the crystalline framework of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs–P}$ . As shown in Figure 1f, sharp peaks are observed in the X-ray diffraction pattern (XRD). The small-angle $(7^{\circ})$ diffraction peaks of HRP $@$ MOFs-P become weak in the presence of PLGA peptide, indicating that the pore diameters change significantly as a result of morphological evolution.[22] Notably, the average pore diameter of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{-}P}$ increased from $6.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ (particle packing or defects) to $20.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ with PLGA peptide through the BarrettJoyner-Halenda (BJH) model using the adsorption branch (Figure  1g), further confirming the generation of mesopores. From the view of the crystallographic structures, MOFs-P with large channel-like pores aperture provided theoretically rational space organization for HRP $(\approx4.0\ \mathrm{{\nm}\times4.4\ \mathrm{{\nm}\times6.8\ \mathrm{{\nm})}}}$ (Figure  S7, Supporting Information).[23,24] We next confined different-sized proteins, including cytochrome C $(\approx3.2\ \mathrm{nm})$ , myoglobin $(\approx3.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ , glucose oxidase $(\approx6.0\ \mathrm{nm})$ and catalase $(\approx11\mathrm{nm})$ , in MOFs-P, confirming the general methods for PLGA regulation (Figure  S8 and Table  S1, Supporting Information). Taken together, the PLGA peptide is significantly important in the formation of mesoporous nanostructures.
Biomacromolecules regulate the kinetic growth of nanomaterials with preferential morphology.[20,25] To comprehensively elucidate the PLGA-dominated regulation mechanism, we observe the morphological evolution dynamics of the assembly in the absence and presence of PLGA peptide through transmission electron microscopy (TEM) probing crystal growth at various time points (Figure  2a). The amorphous particles (average diameter of ${\approx}20~\mathrm{nm}$ ) appear within $\mathrm{t}_{0}+10\ \mathrm{s}$ $(\mathrm{t}_{0}<5$ s) in the absence of PLGA peptide, and these particles display aggregate states when the reaction time lasts $\mathrm{t}_{0}{+}10$ min. The emergence of larger aggregates as the number of smaller complexes decreases. Afterward, the dense aggregate, which serves as a direct precursor for MOFs crystallization, transforms into rhombohedron dodecahedron-like morphology nanostructures at $\mathrm{t}_{0}{+}30$ min and $\mathrm{t}_{0}{+}60$ min (Figure  S9, Supporting Information). Thus, MOF nanocrystals nucleate through two distinct stages: i) the formation of amorphous aggregate and ii) the crystallization of the aggregate into MOFs. Notably, in the presence of PLGA peptide $(70\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ , the heterogeneous amorphous phase constituted of $25{-}40~\mathrm{nm}$ -sized particles was initiated within $\mathrm{t}_{0}{+}10\ \mathrm{s}$ , which is substantially more than that of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ (Figure  2b). The EDS elemental mapping indicated PLGA peptide is conducive to forming heterogeneous amorphous phases by fast aggregating HRP and
$Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ (Figure  S10, Supporting Information). After $\mathrm{t}_{0}{+}10$ min of processing, the heterogeneous amorphous phase gradually condensates into an amorphous aggregation network. We speculate that a local supersaturation state produced by the PLGA peptide facilitates the phase separation of solute-rich phases into a PLGA/protein/HmIm/Zn amorphous phase via a particle aggregation-mediated process. The production of amorphous aggregate drives the heterogeneous nucleation of MOFs-P to undergo a dissolution-recrystallization pathway via Ostwald ripening mechanisms, acceleratingly generating crystal nuclei.[26–29] When the reaction continues to last $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , more crystalline $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ with characteristic nanostar shapes are generated in comparison with $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ . Extensive research has demonstrated that the nucleation rate and subsequent crystal development process can modulate the shape of MOFs.[30–32] The PLGA peptide with abundant carboxylic groups can modify the HRP through electrostatic interaction, which significantly accelerates the formation of amorphous aggregate and crystallization of the aggregate. Meanwhile, the PLGA peptide competitively coordinates with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ nodes to change Zn tetrahedral environment and implement site-selective epitaxial growth,[33–35] followed by the formation of defect crystal nanostructures. The coordination defects caused the loss of long-distance ordering and crystallinity,[36,37] forming suitable pore windows around the enzyme in $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ nanoarchitectures. Harnessing rapid nucleation and crystal defect growth, $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs-P}$ emerge anisotropic features with nanostar shapes.
## 2.2. Activity Evaluation
The produced mesopore voids are critical to achieving optimized apparent activity while maintaining enzyme stability.[47,48] The biocatalytic MOFs-P do not compromise enzyme-shielding capacity against organic reagents and heat (Figure  3h). The embedded enzymes possess enhanced durability during $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ monitoring due to the “stabilizing effect” of MOFs-P. Without protection, free HRP declines $77\%$ , $52\%$ , $72\%$ , and $74\%$ of the enzymatic activity under the conditions of trypsin, urea, N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), respectively (Figure 3h; Figure S6, Supporting Information). For thermal stability, the activity of free HRP plunged by $66\%$ at the temperature of $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ while the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ preserve $77\%$ at $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After exposure to urea, the Soret absorption band at $402\ \mathrm{nm}$ of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ is constant, demonstrating that MOFs-P can maintain the native structure of HRP (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Furthermore, $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ with distinct mesopores possessed good storage stability under different $\mathrm{\pH}$ conditions (Figures  S15 and S16, Supporting Information). Consistent results show that MOFs-P as robust immobilization carriers can hold remarkable stability in biocatalytic reactions. Besides, protein-scaffolded gold nanoclusters with red emission fluorescence are used to replace HRP, which is embedded in MOFs-P and exposed to organic reagents (Figure S17, Supporting Information), demonstrating that high stability may be attributed to maintaining the protein conformation against the external stimulus. These results demonstrate not only that the MOFs-P can protect the HRP but also that HRP and PLGA peptide can stabilize the MOFs-P. Such a PLGA-directed spontaneous process can serve as a general protocol to generate mesopores in MOFs nanostructure, which protects the “guest” biomolecules and maintains their bioactivity during the synthesis. Existing strategies for producing mesopores usually employ templating methods or nonisothermal processing,[49–51] which damaged the activity of enzymes during chemical etching or calcination to remove the template. The microfluidic laminar flow synthesis strategy induced defects in the MOFs nanostructures by precisely changing the ratio of reactants along the microchannel,[52] where most of the biomolecules are located in the subsurface region of the MOFs layer. Because of its moderate condition in a single step without using any template, the approach taken in the current study by using PLGA-regulated morphological evolution and mesopore size is more conducive to enzyme immobilization.
## 2.3. High-Performance of Enzyme@MOFs-P-Enabled Plasmonic Immunosensor
Aiming at high-performance immunosensor, we use $\mathrm{HRP}@$ MOFs-P to label secondary antibodies $(\mathrm{Ab}_{2})$ for antigen-recognition events. Based on the antigen-antibody specific recognition, the $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs–P–Ab}_{2}$ conjugate is anchored on the microtiter plate and proceeds to catalyze substrate for signal output. To optimize the performance of HRP $@$ MOFs-P, different sizes were examined. By simply regulating the concentrations of building blocks (HRP from 0.1 to $10\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}},$ ), the obtained morphological evolution of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs-P}$ is changed within a wide range from 7.9 to $3.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure  4a; Figure  S18, Supporting Information), illustrating the negative correlation between the size and the concentrations of HRP. We propose that high concentrations of biomacromolecule could form more crystal cores with precursors $(Z\mathrm n^{2+}$ ions and 2-methylimidazole), resulting in the production of smaller MOFs-P nanocrystals (Figure  S19, Supporting Information). The XRD, XPS, and FT-IR measurements showed that $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ with different sizes inherited the same characteristic peaks (Figure S20–S22, Supporting Information). The corresponding loading contents of HRP within $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ (Table  S2, Supporting Information) illustrated that tuning the HRP concentration affects the size of HRP $@$ MOFs-P rather than inner structural characteristics. Such size tunability of HRP $@$ MOFs-P provides important guidance for preparing nanostructures with customizable sizes, which revealed the relationship between MOFs-P size and biomacromolecule concentrations, and provided a logical justification for the mechanism underlying nanostructure size. The kinetic parameters (Michaelis-Menten constant) for substrates are also determined, confirming that ${\mathrm{HRP}}@{\mathrm{MOFs-P}}$ with different size restrictions hold similar catalytic efficiency (Figure 4b; Figure S23, Supporting Information). While the immunoassay performance is shown to be significantly improved with the size of $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs-P}$ variation from 3.5 to $7.9~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure  4c). Such a phenomenon could be attributed to the fact that the small-sized HRP $@$ MOFs-P make it easier to generate stable dispersions, reduce nonspecific adsorption, and lessen steric hindrance to antibody recognition, all of which improve binding efficiency. Meanwhile, the undersized ${\mathrm{HRP}}@{\mathrm{MOFs-P}}$ $(3.0~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ that linked a few antibodies might reduce the function of the recognition capacity, which brings about poor immunoassay performance. Accordingly, $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{.}P}$ with the size of $3.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (HRP concentration is $7.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ obtain the highest relative activity, corresponding to the optimal immunosensor configuration of $\mathsf{A b}_{2}$ -managed biological recognition and HRP-assisted signal amplification.
The accuracy and sensitivity of the plasmonic immuosensor are impacted by the instrument error when using traditional monochromatic plasmonic signals mode.[53,54] Gold nanostars with the nature of polarization-dependent scattering and absorption could exhibit a blue shift of absorption peaks when exposed to $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ (Figure 5a; Figure S24, Supporting Information), which is appropriate for dual-color signal output with enzyme modulation. The anisotropic gold nanostars with high surface energy tips are synthesized through the seed-mediated growth method, together with an average diameter is $67~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  5b). Under the catalysis of HRP, hydroxyl radical would be released to oxidize $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ which controls the etching of gold nanostars into spherical gold nanoparticles (Figure  5b), accompanying the shift of absorption wavelength (Figure  5c; Figure  S25, Supporting Information). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping reveals that the multiple protruding tips of gold nanostars are etched by iodide to form spherical nanostructures based on the spatial elemental distribution of Au, S, and I (Figure 5d).
Employing surface etching of gold nanostars, the $\mathrm{HRP}@$ MOFs-P-based immunosensor (designated as HLISA) with dual-color signal output mode is built to detect imidacloprid pesticide (representative analyte). The coating antigen immobilized on the microtiter plate is recognized by the corresponding antibody, which could be competitively combined with imidacloprid to form an antigen-antibody conjugate (Figure  6a). By recognizing the primary antibody, $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs.P{\cdot}A b}_{2}$ as a signal amplifier could oxidize $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ to yield $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , further modulating the size and morphology of gold nanostars. As a result, the indirect competition efficiency $(\mathrm{IE\%})$ of HLISA accordingly increases with the imidacloprid concentrations from 0.001 to $500\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ . The dynamic response range covers five orders of magnitude. The $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ (Half maximal inhibitory concentration) is calculated to be $0.02\ \mathrm{\ng\mL^{-1}}$ , which shows ${\approx}1000\cdot$ - fold improvement in sensitivity compared with that of typical enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay $(\mathrm{IC}_{50}=24.5\ \mathrm{\ng\mL^{-1}})$ (Figure  6b; Figure  S26, Supporting Information). Compared to enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay built on isotropic/anisotropic structural growth and aggregation/disaggregation of nanoparticles,[54–56] surfaces etching-based immunosensor possess higher stability and accuracy because the plasmonic properties are less affected by the subtle changes in environmental conditions and nonspecific aggregation.
Currently, the output of immuosensor signals is dependent on the collection of light intensity or dynamic scanning via a complex optical system, limiting its practical applications in point-ofcare testing.[57,58] Regarding signal transduction of on-site detection, we fabricate a smartphone-based portable reading device to capture color transition signals occurring by means of enzymemediated surface etching of gold nanostars. The miniaturized device integrated with a smartphone, light emitting diode (light source) and microtiter plate, which had only an overall dimension of $215~\mathrm{mm}\times130~\mathrm{mm}\times300~\mathrm{mm}$ (Figure  6c). The top panel of Figure 6d illustrated the true-color images of the detection zones of the plasmonic immuosensor. The platform hold a distinguishable color change from blue to red when the imidacloprid concentration increased from 0.001 to $500~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}$ . To obtain precise information, the acquired true-color image can be split into red (R), green (G) and blue (B) channels that can be separately divided into 256 integer values according to their brightness.[59–61] The B value and G value declined with the increase of imidacloprid concentration, whereas the R value increased, forming a reverse channel intensity change (Figure 6e). The ratio between R intensity and $\mathbf{G}{\times}\mathbf{B}$ intensity is standardized to obtain pseudocolors using an image-processing algorithm (bottom panel of Figure 6d), enhancing the sensitivity via amplifying the response signal. This pseudo-color shift was advantageous to quick testing because an imidacloprid concentration higher than $0.1\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ could generate an apparent change in color from green to red. The ${\mathrm{R}}/({\mathrm{G}}{\times}{\mathrm{B}})$ ratio can be used to quantify imidacloprid concentration with the detection limit $\left(\mathrm{IC}_{20}\right)$ of $0.0013~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}$ using a four-parameter logistic equation (Figure 6f), which was 5-fold lower than other signal processing mode (Figure S27, Supporting Information). When the color is directly determined rather than the light intensity, the ${\mathrm{R}}/({\mathrm{G}}{\times}{\mathrm{B}})$ -based image analysis can improve the accuracy of the platform. Although inferior to the lab-instrument results, the detection sensitivity and detection time of the portable device is comparable to or better than that of previously reported strategies (Table  S3, Supporting Information). Such good performance might be ascribed to the following characteristics: i) Enzyme concentration-directed size tunability of HRP $@$ MOFs-P enables its optimal immunoassay configuration of biological recognition and signal amplification. ii) Surface etching of gold nanostars with plasmonic signal shields the instrument error and facilitates the improvement of detection sensitivity and accuracy. iii) The image-processing algorithm can enhance the accuracy by directly recognizing the color rather than measuring the absorbance intensity. In comparison with the lab-instrumentbased protocols, the HLISA yielded rapid and accurate quantitative results more conveniently. The cross-reaction of the HLISA was investigated by introducing some interferences, including common pesticides, amino acids, and proteins. Even using a 100- fold higher concentration than imidacloprid $(1.0~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}$ ), the $1E\%$ of the interfering components is still ignorable (Figure 6g), suggesting the excellent specificity of the HLISA for imidacloprid recognition. As a validation of the practical usability and reliability, the detection of imidacloprid in food and environmental samples (pear juice, tap water and rice) was carried out. As demonstrated in Table  S4 (Supporting Information), the recoveries based on HLISA were in the range of $86.8\%-110.4\%$ , with a relative standard deviation $(n=3)$ lower than 5.19. In addition, soil samples spiked with unknown imidacloprid quantities (blind samples) were monitored by HLISA and HPLC in parallel. The P value calculated by Student’s t-test between HLISA and HPLC was 0.899, indicating a  non-significant  difference at the 0.05 level (Table S5, Supporting Information). Good correlations were obtained between the HLISA and HPLC $\textstyle\left(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.970\right.$ , Figure 6h), confirming that HLISA holds great potential for pesticide monitoring in real-world applications.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have designed a sensitive plasmonic immunosensor for on-site detection of imidacloprid using self-assembled hierarchically porous HRP@MOFs-P. In this process, the PLGA-induced morphological evolution of MOFs-P is systematically probed, revealing the nucleation and growth mechanism of HRP $@$ MOFs-P. We discovered that PLGA peptide facilitates the phase separation of soluterich phases via a particle aggregation-mediated process, and accelerates the nucleation of MOFs-P around biomacromolecules by creating defect crystal structures to form the mesopores and emerging anisotropic features with nanostarlike shapes. Such hierarchically porous $\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{MOFs\mathrm{-}P}$ could significantly reduce steric hindrance of target recognition and boost the mass transfer process, which provides excellent prospects for improving the utilization efficiency and bioactivity of the enzyme. Employing $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ -mediated surface etching of gold nanostars, an enzyme $@$ MOFs-P-based plasmonic immunosensor with dual-signal output is developed for imidacloprid, possessing excellent stability and accuracy in comparison to single signal output. By combining with a smartphone-based portable device, a detection limit of picogram $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ level is obtained which is up to 1000-fold more sensitive than a conventional immunosensor. This work not only develops an effective method for preparing hierarchically porous MOFs-P nanoarchitectures but also possesses on-site application prospects for biosensors.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of $H R P\ @M O F s.P$ : Typically, HRP with different amounts was dispersed into $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.0~mL}}$ PLGA peptide solution $(70~\mathrm{mg}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ under vigorous stirring for $1.0\ \mathsf{m i n}$ . After that, $2.0~\mathsf{m L}$ zinc acetate solution $(0.1\ m o l\ L^{-1})$ and $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ HmIM solution $(1.2\:m\circ!\:L^{-1}).$ ) were added, aging treatment for $4h$ . The product was collected after centrifuging five times at 9000 rpm to remove the excess proteins.
Preparation of the $H R P@M O F s.P.A b_{2}$ : $50~\upmu\up L$ of NHS $(4.0\ m g\ m L^{-1})$ and $50\upmu\up L$ of EDC $(2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL^{-1}})$ were added to $400\upmu\up L$ of ${\mathsf{H R P}}\ @{\mathsf{M O F s}}.{\mathsf{P}}$ solution. After $30\mathrm{\min}$ , the precipitate was collected by centrifugation treatment at $9000~{\mathsf{r p m}}$ for $5.0\ \mathsf{m i n}$ and dispersed in $450~\upmu\upiota$ of PBS solution (10 mmol $L^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{p H}7.4\}$ ). Then, $50~\upmu\up L$ of $\mathsf{A b}_{2}$ $(2.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ was added, and the reaction solution was incubated in a shaker (800  rpm) at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ and incubated at $4^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterward, ${\mathsf{100~}}\upmu\upiota$ of blocking solution $7\%$ ovalbumin in PBS) was added and vibrated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ . The final product was collected by centrifugation treatment (9000  rpm, 5  min) and washed twice with PBS solution. The product was re-dispersed in $400\upmu\up L$ PBS solution.
Preparation of Gold Nanostars through Seed-Mediated Growth Method: The gold seed solution was prepared as follows: $700~\mathrm{{\mL}}$ of ${\mathsf{H A u C l}}_{4}$ solution $(0.1\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ was heated under reflux with stirring, followed by quickly adding $5.0\ \mathsf{m L}$ of trisodium citrate solution $(70m g\ m L^{-1})$ . The gold seed solution was obtained after stirring for 40 min. The gold nanostars were prepared as follows: $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.0~mL}}$ of gold seed was added to $25.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of HEPES buffer (25 mmol $L^{-1}$ , ${\mathsf{p H}}=7.5)$ ), followed by adding ${\sf120~\mu L}$ of hydroxylamine hydrochloride $100\ m m o l\ L^{-1},$ ). Then, $5.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\mathsf{H A u C l}}_{4}$ solution $(0.8\ m m\circ|\ L^{-1})$ was dropwise added to the above solution. The solution was allowed to stir vigorously $(7200~\mathsf{r p m})$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . The solution was centrifugated at 8000 rpm for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , and the precipitate was re-dispersed in HEPES solution and filtered by a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ nitrocellulose membrane, and kept at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for long-term storage.
The procedure of Plasmonic Immunosensor: $2.50~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ of coating antigens ( $700~\upmu\upiota$ per well) in carbonate-bicarbonate buffer $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{H}=9.6\mathsf{)}$ were incubated in the 96-well microtiter plate overnight at $4~^{\circ}C$ . After washing, $2\%$ ovalbumin $250~\upmu\upiota$ per well) was used to block for 40 min at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . After another washing, serial concentrations of imidacloprid $(50~\upmu\up L$ per well) and $5.0~\upmu\upxi~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ of anti-imidacloprid polyclonal antibodies $50~\upmu\up L$ per well) were introduced into the 96-well microtiter plate and allowed to last for $60~\mathsf{m i n}$ . After washing, HRP $@$ MOFs-P ( $700~\upmu\upiota$ per well) were added and incubated for 40  min and followed by washing three times. Sodium iodide, ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ and citrate buffer $(50~\upmu\up L$ per well) were allowed to proceed for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ The mixture was then taken out for the iodide-mediated etching of gold nanostars. The absorbance spectrum was measured by a microplate reader.
